<main>
  <div class="page-header">
    <h1>Global SST and El Ni&ntilde;o/La Ni&ntilde;a Monitor</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-stack">

    <!-- SST ANOMALY MAPS -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Global &amp; Tropical Pacific SST Anomalies</h2>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=anomaly"
              title="Global and tropical Pacific SST anomaly animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA OISST v2.1 (PSL) &middot; base 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; 60-day animation, latest day shown</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="sst-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ABSOLUTE SST -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Absolute Sea-Surface Temperature</h2>
        <p>The actual SST field (not anomaly), global and tropical Pacific. The warm pool, equatorial cold tongue, and western boundary currents are visible directly; watch them shift day to day.</p>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=absolute"
              title="Absolute SST animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA OISST v2.1 (PSL) &middot; absolute SST &middot; 60-day animation</span>
        <span class="chart-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RELATIVE SST (global-mean removed) -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>SST Anomaly &mdash; Global-Mean Removed</h2>
        <p>The anomaly with the area-weighted global-mean SST anomaly subtracted at every point. This strips out the uniform background-warming signal and isolates the <i>pattern</i> of warming and cooling &mdash; the El Ni&ntilde;o footprint stands out against a more balanced global ocean.</p>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=relative"
              title="Global-mean-removed SST anomaly animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA OISST v2.1 (PSL) &middot; anomaly minus global-mean anomaly &middot; 60-day animation</span>
        <span class="chart-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SUBSURFACE CROSS-SECTION -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Equatorial Pacific Subsurface Temperature</h2>
        <p>Depth&ndash;longitude cross-section along the equator (0&deg;N) from the TAO/TRITON moored-buoy array: temperature (top) and its anomaly versus the 1991&ndash;2020 average (bottom), 5-day smoothed.</p>
        <p><b>What to look for:</b> The thermocline (here the 26&deg;C and 28&deg;C isotherms) tilts upward toward the east. Subsurface warm or cool anomalies along the thermocline often <i>lead</i> the surface ENSO signal &mdash; watch them build and propagate eastward in the animation.</p>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed subsurface" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=equatorial"
              title="Equatorial Pacific subsurface temperature animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA/PMEL TAO/TRITON (DISDEL) &middot; base 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; 120-day animation, latest shown</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="subsurface-updated">__TAO_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- OBSERVED ASCAT EQUATORIAL WINDS -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Observed Equatorial Pacific Surface Wind</h2>
        <p>Latest daily 10&deg;S&ndash;10&deg;N surface wind &mdash; speed (shaded) with direction arrows &mdash; from the gap-filled gridded scatterometer (ASCAT) analysis. The observed companion to the wind forecast below: persistent easterly trades are the neutral/La&nbsp;Ni&ntilde;a state, while westerly bursts along the equator push warm water east and favor El&nbsp;Ni&ntilde;o.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/ascat_winds.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Daily 10°S–10°N Pacific surface wind speed and vectors (ASCAT)" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">Copernicus Marine WIND_GLO_PHY_L4_NRT (scatterometer / ASCAT) &middot; ~1&ndash;2 day latency &middot; updated daily</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- EQUATORIAL 10m WIND HOVMÖLLER -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Equatorial Pacific 10 m Wind Forecast</h2>
        <p>Forecast 10 m zonal-wind anomaly (5&deg;S&ndash;5&deg;N), longitude &times; forecast day, from the AIFS-ENS (AI) and ECMWF IFS-ENS (physics) ensemble means vs. the ERA5 1991&ndash;2020 climatology.</p>
        <p><b>What to look for:</b> Westerly (red) anomalies along the equator push warm water eastward and favor El Ni&ntilde;o development; easterly (blue) anomalies favor La Ni&ntilde;a. Agreement between the AI and physics ensembles raises confidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/eq_wind_hovmoller.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Equatorial Pacific 10 m zonal-wind anomaly Hovmöller forecast (AIFS-ENS and IFS-ENS)" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">ECMWF AIFS-ENS &amp; IFS-ENS (open data) &middot; anomaly vs ERA5 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; updated each 00/12Z cycle</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- SOI FORECAST -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Southern Oscillation Index &mdash; Forecast</h2>
        <p>The Troup SOI (the standardized Tahiti&minus;Darwin sea-level-pressure difference) tracks the atmospheric side of ENSO. Observed values are from LongPaddock (Queensland Govt / BoM); the forecast is the Tahiti&minus;Darwin MSL from the combined AIFS-ENS + IFS-ENS ensemble (~100 members), bias-corrected to the recent observed level. Bold lines are the 30-day running SOI; the faint daily series and shaded 10&ndash;90% band show the noisier day-to-day spread.</p>
        <p><b>How to read it:</b> Sustained <b>negative</b> SOI (below &minus;7) is El Ni&ntilde;o-favorable; sustained <b>positive</b> (above +7) is La Ni&ntilde;a-favorable. A forecast that holds the 30-day SOI deep negative reinforces an El Ni&ntilde;o signal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/soi_forecast.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Southern Oscillation Index: observed (LongPaddock/BoM) plus AIFS-ENS/IFS-ENS forecast" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">LongPaddock daily SOI (BoM, 1933&ndash;1992 base) &middot; forecast: ECMWF AIFS-ENS + IFS-ENS MSL &middot; updated each 00/12Z cycle</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- CANSIPS GEM-NEMO NIÑO-3.4 FORECAST -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>CanSIPS Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 Seasonal Forecast (GEM-NEMO)</h2>
        <p>Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 SST-anomaly forecast plume from the GEM-NEMO component of Environment Canada's CanSIPS seasonal system (ensemble members 1&ndash;20), out ~11 months. <b>Traditional</b> (left) is the Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 anomaly vs the model's own 1991&ndash;2020 hindcast climatology; <b>relative</b> (right) subtracts the 20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N tropical-mean anomaly (RONI-style). Using the model's hindcast climatology removes its drift/bias.</p>
        <p>Bold red is the current issue's ensemble mean (thin red = members); dashed grey is <b>last month's</b> forecast (with its 10&ndash;90% spread) &mdash; where they overlap you can see how the outlook is trending month-to-month.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/cansips_nino34.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="CanSIPS GEM-NEMO Niño-3.4 forecast plume (traditional + relative), current vs previous issue" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">ECCC CanSIPS GEM-NEMO (datamart) &middot; anomaly vs 1991&ndash;2020 model hindcast &middot; updated monthly</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- ATMOSPHERIC ANGULAR MOMENTUM -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Atmospheric Angular Momentum</h2>
        <p>Relative AAM is the angular momentum carried by the winds &mdash; the atmosphere's total westerly momentum, &int;&nbsp;u&middot;cos&sup2;&phi; over the whole globe. It's dominated by the upper-tropospheric subtropical jets and the deep tropics (largest moment arm), shown here for the globe and each hemisphere from the AIFS-ENS 13-level winds.</p>
        <p><b>How to read it:</b> <i>top</i> &mdash; this year's observed AAM (black) and the 15-day forecast (red) on the climatological annual cycle. The hemispheres are strongly out of phase (each peaks in its own winter), so much of the absolute rise/fall is just the season. <i>Bottom</i> &mdash; the anomaly (departure from the 1991&ndash;2020 normal), which removes the seasonal cycle, with a &ldquo;% of climatology&rdquo; axis.</p>
        <p><b>Why it matters for ENSO:</b> El&nbsp;Ni&ntilde;o shifts tropical convection east, strengthens the subtropical jets and weakens the trade winds &mdash; so the atmosphere holds <b>more</b> westerly momentum. AAM also ties to the MJO / Global Wind Oscillation and to <b>length of day</b> (more atmospheric momentum &rarr; the solid Earth spins fractionally slower).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/aam.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Global and hemispheric atmospheric angular momentum: observed + AIFS-ENS forecast, absolute and anomaly" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">Forecast: ECMWF AIFS-ENS (open data, CC BY 4.0) &middot; climatology &amp; this-year observed: ERA5 (Copernicus C3S / ECMWF) &middot; updated daily</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- RONI -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Relative Oceanic Ni&ntilde;o Index (RONI)</h2>
        <p>RONI is an ENSO index that subtracts the tropical-mean SST anomaly (20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N) from the Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 anomaly, then applies a 3-month running mean. By removing the slowly-rising tropical background, it isolates the ENSO signal more cleanly than the conventional ONI &mdash; increasingly useful as overall ocean temperatures trend upward.</p>
        <p><b>How to read it:</b> Red bars (positive) lean El Ni&ntilde;o, blue bars (negative) lean La Ni&ntilde;a; the dashed lines mark the &plusmn;0.5&deg;C guides. This index is computed directly from OISST and shown in &deg;C &mdash; it captures the shape and timing of CPC's RONI but is not their standardized published value.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap">
        <img src="assets/sst/roni.webp?v=__CACHE__"
             alt="Relative Oceanic Niño Index time series" loading="lazy">
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">Computed from NOAA OISST v2.1 &middot; Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 minus tropical-mean &middot; 3-mo running mean</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="roni-updated">__RONI_MONTH__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DAILY 3-METRIC -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Daily Equatorial Pacific SST Indices</h2>
        <p>All three daily metrics on one axis: the Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 anomaly (the basis of ONI), the tropical-mean anomaly (20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N), and their difference &mdash; the daily relative index that underlies RONI. These are single-day values, so they are noisier than the 3-month running indices above, but they show the most recent evolution.</p>
        <p><b>What to look for:</b> When the red (Ni&ntilde;o-3.4) and green (tropical-mean) lines move together, the blue relative line stays flat &mdash; that's background warming, not ENSO. The blue line separating from zero is the genuine ENSO signal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap">
        <img src="assets/sst/daily_indices.webp?v=__CACHE__"
             alt="Daily Niño-3.4, tropical-mean, and relative SST indices" loading="lazy">
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">Daily, computed from NOAA OISST v2.1 &middot; year-to-date</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="daily-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- EVENT COMPARISON: Niño-3.4 evolution overlay -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>This Event vs. 1997, 2015 &amp; 2023</h2>
        <p>Niño-3.4 evolution (7-day running mean) through each event's development year and the next. Bold lines are <b>RONI</b> &mdash; the relative index (Niño-3.4 minus the 20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N tropical-mean anomaly), which removes the rising warming background so events decades apart are comparable; faint dotted lines are the raw <b>ONI</b> for reference. The current event is red. Note how 2015's record ONI shrinks toward 1997 once the background is removed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/events_nino34.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Niño-3.4 RONI vs ONI evolution: current event vs 1997/2015/2023" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">OISST v2.1 daily &middot; 7-day mean &middot; RONI = Niño-3.4 &minus; 20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N mean</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- EVENT COMPARISON: multi-region flavor bars -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>El Niño Flavor &mdash; Niño Regions vs. Analogs</h2>
        <p>Relative Niño-1+2 / 3 / 3.4 / 4 anomalies (7-day mean, each minus the 20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N tropical mean) at the same calendar day as the latest data, for the current event and the three analogs. Removing the tropical background makes the regions comparable across events; strong eastern (1+2, 3) relative to central (4) marks an East-Pacific-flavored event.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/events_bars.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Relative Niño-region anomalies, current event vs analogs" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">OISST v2.1 daily &middot; 7-day mean &middot; relative to 20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N mean</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- EVENT COMPARISON: matching-phase maps -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Matching-Phase SST Anomaly Maps</h2>
        <p>Relative SST anomaly &mdash; the area-weighted global-mean anomaly removed from each map &mdash; for the latest available week vs. the same calendar week in 1997, 2015 and 2023. Stripping the global mean removes the secular warming trend, so the ENSO spatial pattern is comparable across events that sat on very different baselines.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/events_maps.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Matching-phase relative SST anomaly maps (global-mean removed)" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">OISST v2.1 daily &middot; 7-day mean &middot; global-mean removed</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- SUBSURFACE ANALOG: heat content -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Subsurface Heat Content vs. Analogs</h2>
        <p>Equatorial Pacific upper-ocean (0&ndash;300 m) temperature anomaly &mdash; a heat-content proxy that leads the surface &mdash; through each event's development year and the next, current (bold red) overlaid on 1997, 2015 and 2023. The subsurface warm reservoir is the fuel for El Ni&ntilde;o; watch whether the current build-up keeps pace with the analogs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/subsurface_events_hc.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Equatorial 0–300 m temperature-anomaly evolution: current vs 1997/2015/2023" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">NOAA/PMEL TAO/TRITON moorings &middot; anomaly vs 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; coverage varies by year</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- SUBSURFACE ANALOG: matching-phase cross-sections -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Subsurface Cross-Section vs. Analogs</h2>
        <p>Equatorial depth&times;longitude temperature anomaly at the same phase (~the latest data week) of each event. The eastward-deepening warm anomaly along the thermocline is the classic El Ni&ntilde;o subsurface signature; comparing its depth and intensity against 1997/2015/2023 gauges how loaded the ocean is now.</p>
        <p><b>Note:</b> TAO mooring coverage varies by year (triangles mark moorings reporting), so 1997 and 2023 have missing longitudes.</p>
